      "findingId": "reentrancy-withdraw",
      "title": "Reentrancy in withdraw allows vault drainage",
      "severity": "high",
      "description": "The withdraw function performs an external ether transfer to msg.sender via .call before updating the sender's balance. If the tx.origin check is satisfied (e.g., via a phishing transaction initiated by the owner), an attacker-controlled contract can reenter withdraw repeatedly and drain the vault because the accounting update occurs after the external call.",
      "evidence": {
        "file": "VulnerableVault.sol",
        "lines": "18-20",
        "snippet": "        (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}(\"\");\n        require(ok, \"transfer failed\");\n        balances[msg.sender] -= amount;"
      },
      "exploitScenario": "1. Attacker deploys a malicious contract and deposits a small amount into the vault. 2. Attacker tricks the vault owner into sending a transaction to the malicious contract, making tx.origin == owner. 3. The malicious contract calls withdraw(). The external .call triggers the attacker's receive/fallback function before the balance is reduced. 4. The fallback function reenters withdraw(), repeating the withdrawal with the same uncredited balance until the vault's Ether is fully drained.",
      "recommendation": "Implement the Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern by reducing balances[msg.sender] before the external call, and add a reentrancy guard (e.g., OpenZeppelin ReentrancyGuard).",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "findingId": "tx-origin-access-control",
      "title": "Insecure tx.origin authorization in withdraw",
      "severity": "high",
      "description": "The withdraw function uses tx.origin == owner for access control instead of msg.sender. This enables phishing attacks where the owner is tricked into executing a transaction that calls an attacker-controlled intermediary, which then calls withdraw() on the vault. It also breaks compatibility with smart contract wallets and multisigs.",
      "evidence": {
        "file": "VulnerableVault.sol",
        "lines": "16",
        "snippet": "        require(tx.origin == owner, \"not owner\");"
      },
      "exploitScenario": "The vault owner is tricked into interacting with a malicious dApp. The dApp calls VulnerableVault.withdraw(), satisfying the tx.origin check. Combined with the reentrancy vulnerability, the attacker can repeatedly withdraw and drain all funds held by the vault.",
      "recommendation": "Replace tx.origin with msg.sender for authorization. If the intent is owner-only withdrawal, use require(msg.sender == owner, ...).",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "findingId": "locked-user-deposits",
      "title": "Non-owner deposits are permanently locked",
      "severity": "high",
      "description": "The deposit function is open to any address, but withdraw requires tx.origin == owner. Consequently, any user who is not the owner can deposit Ether but can never withdraw it, resulting in a permanent, unrecoverable loss of funds for depositors.",
      "evidence": {
        "file": "VulnerableVault.sol",
